export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>To this day, disintegration is still a common way to dispose of character meshes, as demonstrated in this GDC talk for <em>God of War</em>. This method saves time on animating the demise of game characters, and removing meshes also benefits performance.</p>\n<p>However, some games can't opt for this route because they have a more realistic presentation, meaning their characters pass out the way real people do. In this case, they turn to ragdoll physics.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c227c6910ffc7f99a32d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c227c6910ffc7f99a32d.webp\" width=\"687\" height=\"386\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/38972831d2b1fd5683f4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/38972831d2b1fd5683f4.webp\" width=\"693\" height=\"386\" style=\"--source-width:262px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/6fe59ce275db9891d41a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/6fe59ce275db9891d41a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"642\" style=\"--source-width:262px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Ragdoll physics attempts to simulate the workings of a humanoid body. It starts with establishing a network of interconnected bones and joints akin to the human skeleton. Being a simulation, this system allows for unscripted interactions, which don't require manual authoring by animators. To keep this article workplace-friendly and avoid morbid implications, we will use falling as an example.</p>\n<p>When people crash and fall, they largely lose control over their bodies. Following this principle, when ragdoll-compatible characters tumble, their skeletons are deactivated, leading them to collapse autonomously, their bodies making contact with the surroundings without any preset input. Thanks to its procedural nature, ragdoll physics saves plenty of time for animators, but the trade-off, in turn, lays heavily on programmers to calibrate the physics engine.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Although ragdoll physics is most prominently featured for animating character dying, it has developed several other uses over time. For <em>Grand Theft Auto V</em> and <em>Red Dead Redemption 2</em>, the developers configured their ragdolls so that when characters fall midair, they still retain some degree of control over their skeletons. The result is a mix of arm bracing, limbs flailing, bodies twisting, as the characters unfortunately dive into their graves.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b9c69f551e1b65d8b024.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b9c69f551e1b65d8b024.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/7199e4edb9304fbd23e1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/7199e4edb9304fbd23e1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/f428399466888e90c17c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/f428399466888e90c17c.webp\" width=\"687\" height=\"386\" style=\"--source-width:262px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>A well-known example of ragdoll physics used on living characters is <em>Star Wars</em> games where you play as a Jedi. In <em>Star Wars: The Force Unleashed, </em>ragdoll physics is implemented to animate enemies strangled by Force Grip.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>A similar use case, though not on humanoid characters, comes from <em>Dishonored</em>. Since rats are a crucial game mechanic, other abilities in the game have to interact with them to varying degrees. There is an ability called \"Wind Blast,\" which launches an explosive gust forward, dispersing all enemies along its path.</p>\n<p>Initially, the developers intended to make the rats explode in response to the skill's impact, but they quickly realized it wasn't a sound idea. In the end, they settled on using ragdoll physics on the rats, essentially turning them into miniature humans.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/907e43e296d9f1973585.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/907e43e296d9f1973585.webp\" width=\"674\" height=\"376\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b940cff3581ddc46995c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b940cff3581ddc46995c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:262px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9b8ddc6fb8b4411add07.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9b8ddc6fb8b4411add07.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:262px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Perhaps, one of the most inventive uses of ragdoll physics in video games is <em>Human: Fall Flat</em>. The premise of this game revolves around handling the arms of the player character—separately—to traverse the environments and solve puzzles.</p>\n<p>By exaggerating basic locomotion, <em>Human: Fall Flat</em> lends a floaty and bouncy feel to its controls, making even the simple act of moving about a challenge at times. In return, it creates countless moments of pure hilarity, which are equal parts frustrating and entertaining.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
